::: {.doc-subject}

Add a tadpole graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L9513-L9644){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_tadpole_graph(
    m,
    n,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Tadpole graph](../assets/graphs/add-tadpole-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three tadpole graphs side by side: a triangle with a one-node tail for m=3 and n=1, a square with a two-node tail, and a hexagon with a two-node tail. Unlike the lollipop, the head is a plain cycle, so every head node but the one holding the tail has two neighbors." width="100%"}

Creates a cycle ("head") of `m` nodes with a path ("tail") of `n` more nodes hanging off
one of them. The graph has `m + n` nodes and `m + n` edges, so it is connected with
exactly one cycle (a unicyclic graph). With `n=0` it is just the cycle. Tadpoles are
also called dragon or pan graphs (`n=1` gives the pan).

The cycle is created first and the tail follows, attached to the cycle's last node. In a
directed graph the cycle runs in one direction (each node to the next, the last back to
the first) and the tail points away from the cycle. The graph is appended without
touching or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a
single step.

Tadpole graphs are useful for:

- test cases for cycle detection and girth, where the cycle sits off to one side
- the sparse counterpart of the lollipop graph (see `add_lollipop_graph()`), for
  comparing measures on a ring with a tail
- modeling a loop with a single access road (a circular route plus a spur line)
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[m]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the cycle. Must be at least `3`.

<code>[n]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in the tail. Must be non-negative.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label
    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the
    nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `m=` is less than `3` or `n=` is negative.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_lollipop_graph`) 

:   Add a clique with a path hanging from it.

[](`~add_cycle_graph`) 

:   Add a cycle graph.

[](`~girth`) 

:   Compute the length of a shortest cycle.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A tadpole graph is a cycle of `m` nodes with a path of `n` nodes, the tail, hanging from
it. A cycle of five with a tail of two has as many edges as nodes:

::: {#03f367df .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_tadpole_graph(m=5, n=2)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(7, 7)
```
:::
:::


The tail hangs from node `"5"`, which therefore has degree `3`, and the graph's only cycle
has length `m=5`:

::: {#12bcef17 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
graph.get_degree("5"), graph.girth()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(3, 5)
```
:::
:::


### A ring or a clique

A tadpole and a lollipop differ only in their heads. With a head of four and a tail of
three, the tadpole on the left has `7` edges and its shortest cycle is the whole ring of
four. The lollipop on the right has two more edges, the diagonals of the square, which make
triangles:

::: {#eb8f0386 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
lollipop = ec.Graph().add_lollipop_graph(m=4, n=3)
tadpole = ec.Graph().add_tadpole_graph(m=4, n=3)

(
    tadpole.count_edges(),
    lollipop.count_edges(),
    tadpole.girth(),
    lollipop.girth(),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(7, 9, 4, 3)
```
:::
:::


::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#97cccb24 .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
tadpole.layout_kamada_kawai().plot(
    layout="pos", label_by="label", node_size=10,
    width=320, height=320, legend=False,
)
```

